  "exclusions": {
    "_comment": "WHAT THIS IS FOR CHANGED. It used to be the only thing standing between a driver package and someone's Twitch category, which is a job a denylist cannot do - a real session produced three false positives in one morning (Opera GX, Wallpaper Engine, Microsoft 365) with 47 more of the same kind already indexed and waiting. Uncurated Uninstall-registry entries are now prompt-only by default (CategoryResolver::isPromptOnly), so nothing here is load-bearing for correctness any more. These entries suppress the QUESTION for things nobody would ever stream, so the prompt stays rare enough to be worth reading. basenames/pathFragments skip a running process before the poll pays for OpenProcess. platformIds/displayNames/installRootFragments keep the entry out of the install index entirely. Prefer platformIds - a Steam appid is stable forever, whereas a displayName carrying a version number is a rule with an expiry date; use a trailing '*' for anything versioned. TO ADD AN APP: put its process name in basenames and its Programs-and-Features name (ending in *) in displayNames - nothing else to change, and HelperDenylist::BuiltInFallback() mirrors the seed content. Developer tools (VS Code, Visual Studio, JetBrains IDEs), terminals, file managers and everyday apps are listed here on purpose: a code editor left open is not a stream. Creative apps such as Photoshop, Krita and FL Studio stay detectable and are mapped to real Twitch categories in aliases.json.",
